     "result_text_tail": "pointed out that the plan's 25% ad cost conflicts with the pack's own 32% benchmark. The deck now uses the pilot's 28% as the base case, and payback is stated as conditional rather than certain.\n- **Fixtures:** in-store fixtures are counted as an operating cost. Treating them as capital spending would still leave retail-led below the margin floor.\n\nSince you were away, I made these calls myself:\n- **Audience:** CEO and board deciding to approve a route, as the brief states.\n- **Checkpoints:** I approved the storyline myself. The data-gaps checkpoint wasn't needed because nothing was missing.\n- **$40M ambition:** I read it as a target for 2030 revenue.\n- **Awareness measure:** Onsoo's 4% among all survey respondents is the closest match to the retail study's \"category buyers\".\n- **Japan:** shown as operating profit, because the pack gives no inventory inputs for Japan.\n- **Tariff case:** assumes a 25% duty from 2028 onward.\n- **Backup slides:** two figures circulated to the board are flagged as unsafe to rely on. The $5.2B trade-press market size includes makeup and hair care, and the 0.71 doors-to-Amazon correlation doesn't show cause.\n\nOne thing the pack can't answer: the cost of a small retail door test. The deck recommends scoping it before committing.\n\nI can switch the theme, or rerun the numbers with a different base ad cost or a different reading of the $40M ambition.\n\nFiles are in `deliverable/`:\n- onsoo-us-entry.pptx\n- onsoo-us-entry-sources.xlsx\n- onsoo-us-entry.json"
    }
